  "worked_solution": "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(y-x)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$, $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y$, and $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}\u2061x+{cos}^{2}\u2061x=1\\,\u27f9\\,{sin}^{2}\u2061x=1-{cos}^{2}\u2061x\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061x & =1-{cos}^{2}\u2061x \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{4}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{16}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(y-x) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\  & =(\\frac{12}{13})(-\\frac{4}{5})-(\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{65}-\\frac{15}{65} \\\\  & =-\\frac{63}{65}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
